# Saturn backup images — never touch these bytes (a Windows clone with
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise risk mangling any that look like text).
*.sav binary
*.bcr binary
*.bkr binary

# Batch files are only ever run by cmd.exe, which wants CRLF. autocrlf=true
# already gives a Windows checkout that, but say it outright so a clone with
# autocrlf off (or one made on Linux and copied over) gets it too.
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are run by dash on Ubuntu / WSL, which rejects a CR at the end
# of a line ("Syntax error: word unexpected", pointing at the wrong thing). A
# Git-for-Windows clone with the installer's default core.autocrlf=true would
# otherwise hand the WSL half of the same machine an unrunnable
# `deno task sav:inject` — and one Surface Pro is both target machines at once.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf

# ...and the other way for the launchers cmd.exe reads.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Super Famicom ROMs, the emulator core that runs them and the eShop
# archive they ship in. Same reasoning as the Saturn images above: git's
# is-it-text guess only looks at the first 8000 bytes, and a ROM that
# guessed wrong under core.autocrlf would present as a black screen
# rather than as a corrupt file.
*.sfc binary
*.smc binary
*.wasm binary
*.data binary
*.zip binary
